WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.340
SPEAKER: Welcome to an extended
episode of the "What's What--

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:04.990
Service Accounts" series.

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:07.650
In this video, I'm going to
demonstrate a few of the things

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:10.500
that we talked about in some
of the previous episodes.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.390
If you haven't caught
those yet, check them

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:13.950
out in the description.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.740
Without further ado, let's see
service accounts in action.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:25.287
For this first
demonstration, I'm

00:00:25.287 --> 00:00:27.870
going to show you how to create
a compute instance with a user

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.700
managed service
account attached to it

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.680
and how a user with
access to this instance

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.700
is able to use that
service account.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.510
In this example, in addition
to the project viewer role,

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.180
my user account is granted
the compute admin role

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:44.050
on the project, so I can create
and manage compute instances.

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:46.390
I also have the role
service account user ,

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:49.510
but only for a service
account called bucket admin,

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:51.730
and not the entire project.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:54.940
The bucket admin service
account has a role storage admin

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:58.570
assigned to it that allows it to
create and manage GCS buckets.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:01.750
OK, let's start by creating a
compute instance by going over

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:04.030
here in Compute Engine.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.360
Create.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.730
If you scroll down
here, this is where

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.690
you'll indicate
what service account

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:12.440
you want to use for
your compute instance.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.660
So let's pick one that I
don't have any access to.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.530
Let's pick sequel
admin and hit create.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.720
And you'll see that
this isn't going

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.190
to work because I have no roles
assigned to my user account

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.830
over this service account.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:29.850
And there it goes.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:32.040
You hover over this
little red circle.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:34.560
It says it right there that I
need the service account user

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:35.920
role on that service account.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.700
So let's just delete this
and create a new instance

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.170
with the right service account.

00:01:43.353 --> 00:01:45.270
So once again, we're
going to scroll down here

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.540
and this time I'm going to
pick the bucket admin service

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.160
account, which I
do have access to.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.980
I am the service account user
for that service account.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:55.605
So give that a second
while it creates.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:01:59.800
Boom, there it is.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.790
Green check mark means that
the instance is running.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.850
So next I'm going to show
you that I don't have access

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:08.370
to create a bucket.

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:14.390
Using GS Util down here, I'm
going to try to make a bucket

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:18.030
and this should fill.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:19.580
And yes, access denied.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.330
So I don't have access
to create a bucket,

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:24.135
but I do have access to
SSH into this instance.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:36.270
And now that I'm in there, I
can show you with gcloud offlist

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.370
that the credential
account isn't me.

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:40.330
It's the service account
attached to the VM

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.480
and there it is bucket admin.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.327
So if I'm admin,
that means I should

00:02:44.327 --> 00:02:46.410
be able to create a bucket,
even though I couldn't

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.390
do that as myself before.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:49.500
So let's try that.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:50.910
Gsutil.

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.300
Make bucket.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:54.020
A new bucket.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.350
And there it is.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:03.920
No error message, and that's
because I created a bucket

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:05.840
as a service account.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.270
Note that I only needed
the service account user

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:11.090
role to attach the service
account to the instance.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:14.060
But to use a service account
from the compute instance,

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.638
I just needed to
have SSH access.

00:03:16.638 --> 00:03:18.680
That's really important
to keep in mind for users

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:21.920
to have access to your VMs.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.345
For this next demonstration,
I'm going to generate a key

00:03:24.345 --> 00:03:26.720
and show you how I can use it
to act as a service account

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.250
to access resources.

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:30.470
This time, I'm going to
be a user with a viewer

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:32.060
role in the project,
and the service

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.640
account key Admin role granted
only on a service account

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.650
called SQL admin.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:39.500
That service account has a
Cloud SQL Admin role sign,

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:42.080
so it's able to create
new Cloud SQL instances

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.842
and manage any that are
already in the project.

00:03:44.842 --> 00:03:46.550
If you wanted to create
a service account

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:49.430
key through the Cloud console,
here's how you do that.

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:53.710
Under IM in Admin, service
accounts and hit that menu

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:56.210
right next to the account that
you want to create a key for.

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:58.448
There should be a create
key option right there.

00:03:58.448 --> 00:03:59.990
But for this
demonstration, I'm going

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:03.080
to use the Cloud Shell, someone
to create a key down here.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:08.300
$ gcloud, iam, service
accounts, keys create,

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:12.770
followed by a file name, and
then we're going to indicate

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:15.110
the iam account that we
want to create a key for.

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:17.930
So remember this will only work
if you have the service account

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:20.269
key admin role
assigned and since I

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:22.760
don't have that
role assigned to me,

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:26.090
project wide, if I put a service
account I don't have access

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:29.270
to budget admin, it won't work.

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:37.350
See?

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:39.130
Permission denied.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:40.620
So let's put in
a service account

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:44.250
that I do have that
role signed for

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.170
and that would be a SQL admin.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:53.440
And that worked so now
the private key was

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.740
saved to that key.json file
I indicated in the command.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:00.610
You can check the keys that
already exists with Gcloud IAM

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:05.980
service-accounts keys list,
followed by the IAM account

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:08.410
flag and the service
account that you

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:10.990
want to see a list of keys for.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.880
The output for this is going to
include the key I just created

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:24.892
as well as a system manage key
that was added when the service

00:05:24.892 --> 00:05:25.725
account was created.

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:31.710
So now that we have a key, let's
see what I can do with that.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:34.250
So first, let me just
show you that I do not

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:37.760
have any roles or permissions
that allow me to create a Cloud

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:38.480
SQL instance.

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:42.770
So let's try to
create one as myself.

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:45.380
And this should fail.

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:46.897
Yes.

00:05:46.897 --> 00:05:48.230
So I don't have that permission.

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:50.180
But my service account does.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:52.190
So how do I use it?

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:57.230
Gcloud auth activate
service account followed

00:05:57.230 --> 00:05:59.300
by the service account name.

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:07.180
And then indicate the key file.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:08.170
That was key.json.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:12.260
Boom.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:14.300
Activated service
account credentials.

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:16.940
So to verify that,
Gcloud auth list

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:20.780
will show that SQL admin is
the active account right now.

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:25.550
And that means now if I try to
create another SQL instance,

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:26.450
it should work.

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:32.520
And success.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:35.450
So remember, the service
account key admin role was only

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:37.340
used to create the key.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:39.830
A user with as little
as a project viewer role

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:41.270
will still be able
to use that key

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:43.040
to act as a service account.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:45.290
So be sure to have a really
secure way of distributing

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:49.275
that key and a decent key
rotation process in place.

00:06:49.275 --> 00:06:51.650
This last demonstration is
going to be like the last one,

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:53.567
except I'm going to be
impersonating a service

00:06:53.567 --> 00:06:55.870
account without keys.

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:59.140
My user account will only have
the viewer role in the project.

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:02.620
On the IAM policy of the
service account SQL admin,

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:05.440
I'm going to have the service
account token creator role

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:06.970
and nothing else.

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:08.992
Let's see what I
can do with that.

00:07:08.992 --> 00:07:10.450
So for this
demonstration, I'm just

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:12.490
going to delete the
database instance that I

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:14.480
created in the last example.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:17.560
So I'll try to do
that first as myself.

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:24.630
So Gcloud SQL instances delete
followed by the instance name.

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:27.400
Hit Yes.

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:28.120
And there you go.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:30.580
I am not authorized
to make that request.

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:32.270
But now let me run
the same command.

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:35.590
Except this time, I'm going
to add the impersonate service

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:38.320
account flag followed by
the service account name.

00:07:43.828 --> 00:07:44.745
Do I want to continue?

00:07:44.745 --> 00:07:47.942
I'm going to say yes.

00:07:47.942 --> 00:07:50.150
And as you can see, something
is happening this time.

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:57.570
And the Cloud SQL
instance is deleted.

00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:00.240
And I was able to do that
without having any roles having

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:03.060
to do with Cloud SQL assigned
to me and without the use

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:05.010
of any service account keys.

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:08.640
All I had was a service account
token creator role on that one

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:11.340
service accounts IAM policy.

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:13.200
The impersonate
service account flag

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:16.230
works on any Gcloud command
and is the most straightforward

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:18.780
example of how you can use the
service account token creator

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:20.040
role.

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:23.130
The role also lets users
create short lived credentials

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:25.590
and generate service
account access tokens

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:28.590
so they can send requests
to the Google Cloud REST API

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:30.690
as a service account.

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:32.970
Well, that's that
for "What's What."

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:34.320
What do you think of the series?

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:36.960
Do you have any other questions
about service accounts?

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.490
Feel free to let us know
in the comments below.

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:41.280
And to learn more
about service accounts,

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:47.130
visit cloud.google.com
/iam/docs/service-accounts.

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:48.060
Thanks for watching.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:48.990
And see you next time.

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:53.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

